\section{Installing}{

Students install the package once, at the start of term:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{remotes::install_github("ubco-biology/biol202_ubco")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Note that the \emph{repository} is \code{biol202_ubco} but the \emph{package} is \code{biol202}.
The names differ because R does not permit underscores in package names. So
the install line and the library line do not match, and that is expected:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(biol202)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Installing \code{biol202} also installs every other package used in the course, so
there is nothing else to install.
}

\section{Loading packages in this course}{

The tutorials load the individual packages they use, rather than loading
\code{tidyverse} as a whole:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(readr)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This is deliberate and consistent all term. It means you can always tell
which package a function came from, and when something goes wrong the error
message points somewhere you can look. Note that \code{library(biol202)} does not
load \code{dplyr} or \code{ggplot2} for you -- you still load those yourself, as above.
}

\section{A note for maintainers -- the expected R CMD check NOTE}{

\verb{R CMD check} reports:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{Namespaces in Imports field not imported from: ... All declared Imports
should be used.
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This NOTE is expected and should not be "fixed". The packages in \code{Imports}
are not used by this package's own R code; they are declared so that
installing \code{biol202} installs everything the \emph{students} need for the
tutorials, in one step. That is the whole point of the package: one install
at the start of term instead of 280 students each debugging a package
install in week 1.

The alternative placements are worse. \code{Suggests} would not be installed by
default by \code{remotes::install_github()}, defeating the purpose. \code{Depends}
would attach every package on \code{library(biol202)}, which contradicts the
teaching convention above.
}

\section{Datasets}{

Run \code{data(package = "biol202")} to list the bundled datasets, and use \verb{?} for
any of them (for example \code{?birds}) to read its documentation. Those help
pages are the course's data dictionary: each records units, variable
meanings, missing values, and provenance.

Datasets are rebuilt from their raw CSV sources by \code{data-raw/make_data.R}.
}

